WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.136 --> 00:00:10.510
JULIA FERRAIOLI:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:11.160
How's it going?

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:17.710
Welcome to the machine
learning session at GCP Next.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:19.690
Thanks for coming.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.340
This session is all about
how you as developers

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.070
can add elements of intelligence
to your own applications.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.800
I like to think of
it as a superpower,

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.520
because the capabilities
of machine learning far

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:35.320
outstripped what any one
of us can do on our own.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.150
My name is Julia Ferraioli.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:40.190
I'm a Developer Advocate
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.150
Back in the day, I did some
machine learning research,

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.510
but it's been awhile.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.090
I know just enough
to be dangerous.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:50.060
Later, I'll be joined
by David from Wix.com,

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.600
who will talk about
their experiences

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.675
with Google Cloud platform's
machine learning capabilities.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.480
Now I suspect that the machine
learning plus Google story

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.360
makes a lot of sense
to many of you.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.500
It's right there in
the mission statement,

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.890
to organize the
world's information.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.940
And information is just
another way of saying data,

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.779
and data is oxygen
for machine learning.

00:01:15.779 --> 00:01:17.320
Anything that you
do with your system

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.540
can be considered data,
whether it be logs,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.562
behavior, uploads, et cetera.

00:01:23.562 --> 00:01:25.020
And that data is
something that you

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.380
can get greater insight from.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.160
How you use that
data and how you

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.720
extract knowledge
and understanding

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.030
can determine the
degree of your success.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.540
So what is machine learning?

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.890
Who here is an expert
in machine learning?

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:42.810
Yeah, OK.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.470
There are a few of you.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:45.860
Excellent.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.230
I am not one of them.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.836
It's been the domain of the few.

00:01:49.836 --> 00:01:52.180
You needed a Ph.D.
to dig into it,

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.260
and you needed to dig into
it to make use of it at all.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:57.960
But understanding
the machine learning

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.320
concepts doesn't
require a Ph.D. You

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.750
can think of it as
taking data, running it

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:10.690
through an algorithm, to
yield some sort of insight.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.050
That's it.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.104
The more data you
have, the better.

00:02:14.104 --> 00:02:16.270
And of course, you need
some place to store the data

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:17.320
and run the algorithm.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.430
So infrastructure
is an integral part

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.455
of a machine learning system.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.610
Over the years, we've had
to solve new problems.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.160
And oftentimes, we've made use
of machine learning techniques

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.410
to do this.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.490
So let's take a
brief look at how

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.140
we use machine learning Google.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.280
I'm going to take two examples.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.930
Who here uses Google Photos?

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:47.670
So not terribly long ago,
we added the capability

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:52.010
to search through your photos,
not using when it was taken

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.390
or where it was taken
or anything that you

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.150
can find in the EXIF data.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.310
But we added the
capability to search

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.990
within the photos themselves.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:07.010
So if you look for
photos of flowers,

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:11.520
you'll be able to get returned
photos that contain flowers.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.290
And this comes from an
application of machine learning

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:18.350
that actually attempts to
comprehend what is going on

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.960
in the pixels themselves.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.130
What story does it tell?

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.110
Google Translate
is a great example

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:29.410
of an application that's built
almost entirely on machine

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:30.250
learning.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:31.850
It's powered by an
area of research

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:34.420
called machine translation.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:37.240
And we build up a
statistical language model

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.740
so that lets us translate
from one language to another.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.720
So you can get all of your
translation questions answered,

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.580
like how to say there are
too many pictures of cats

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:52.300
on the internet in Italian.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.160
I'm going to use that when
I go to Italy, I'm sure.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.890
So what can we as developers
do with machine learning?

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:04.110
You're hearing a lot about
it, especially yesterday

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.740
and again since
you're here today.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:12.280
Well I like to think
of it as a spectrum.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:15.870
Now on the left hand side
here, we have TensorFlow.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:19.029
Who here has played
around with TensorFlow?

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:20.700
A few of you.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:25.210
So TensorFlow is meant
primarily for researchers,

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.280
or folks in academia.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.700
It needs more background,
more understanding of machine

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.330
learning and machine
learning techniques,

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:37.320
and it means you're going
to be writing more code.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.740
Now on the right hand side, we
have the machine learning APIs.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.210
Now, these let you access
fully trained models

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:47.600
that we've developed
as a service.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:51.491
It's meant for people
building applications.

00:04:51.491 --> 00:04:53.570
Cloud machine
learning, which is what

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:56.120
you heard about in
the keynote yesterday,

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:59.900
is kind of the middle and
it extends to either side.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.690
It lets you get
hands-on building

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:05.370
and training your own
models using your own data.

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.240
And since it's an integral
part of Google Cloud Platform,

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.750
it also gives you easy access
to other cloud services,

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.520
like storage and
BigQuery and data flow.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:22.780
So let's take a brief
look at TensorFlow

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:27.040
to give you a little bit of a
basis of understanding there.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.790
So it was released
last year by Google,

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.940
the folks at Google Research.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.730
And it's an open-source
machine learning library.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.540
So right now, it's one of the
most popular machine learning

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:43.650
libraries on GitHub by
forks and stars et cetera.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.760
And it's meant for
numerical computation.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:50.980
What we're seeing people do
is use it in-- the most part,

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:52.520
for deep learning.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:55.030
And deep learning is
an area of research

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:58.290
that attempts to answer
problems that we always

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:02.780
thought you needed human
intelligence to do.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.870
And oftentimes we do that
using neural networks.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.600
So TensorFlow lets you do
things like run recurrent

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.773
neural networks or
convolutional neural networks.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.160
So let's take a look at
cloud machine learning.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.080
And we've got a pretty
fun demo for you to today.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:24.960
So as I said before,
it really does span

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:26.265
the spectrum of our platform.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:30.570
And what does it do?

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.990
Well first of all, think
about your own data.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:37.250
Think about what answer's you
know that your data holds,

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.310
you're not quite sure how
to get access to them.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:42.640
Well, using cloud
machine learning,

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.550
you can really extract that
knowledge and understanding

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:47.245
that I was talking about.

00:06:47.245 --> 00:06:49.380
You use it when you
have a customized

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.700
problem that you want to solve.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.390
It gives you tools for
the entire process,

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:01.740
from ingesting data to
creating and training a model,

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.760
and then predicting it's scale.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:07.110
Now, if you're
familiar with Datalab,

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.570
which is built on
top of Jupiter,

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:12.570
you can use it to get the
rich integrated environment

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.860
in training and
managing your models.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.540
But what can it do?

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:20.280
Right now, we have
the capability

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:24.990
to perform two primary tasks,
regression and classification.

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:27.000
But what does that mean?

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:32.670
So let's say that you are
running social media analytics.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:36.960
And for each piece of social
media, you have a few things.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.170
You have the text,
when it was published,

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:44.550
a number of interactions,
the responses, et cetera.

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:47.620
Now, if you used all
of those features

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:51.290
to predict-- like an
efficacy score, how effective

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:53.420
was this piece of social media.

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:56.690
That would be an example
of a regression task.

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:00.620
You use those features
to get a value.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:02.770
But if you took
those same features,

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:07.930
and used it to tell you--
well the subject of that piece

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:11.060
of social media, that would be
an example of a classification

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:13.190
task.

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:16.950
So let's take a
concrete example.

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:19.550
So let's say, you
were a biologist.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:23.570
And I say this with the caveat
that I am not a biologist.

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:27.550
And you have a bunch
of images of viruses.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:29.200
And you want to
automatically sort them

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.280
into different classes.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.280
Now, you might be
able to manually look

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.140
at all of these images.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:40.250
And say, oh, yeah that's
strain A and that's strain B.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:45.890
But it's kind of hard to
develop the criteria for what

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.960
makes strain a
different from strain

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.920
B. We're actually hampered
by our own ability

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:51.840
to pattern match.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.700
We can't analyze enough.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:57.540
This is where machine learning
can really help us out.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:02.990
So let's use this
scenario to dig

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:06.100
into a demo that answers
the question that I

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:09.460
know everybody here
asks themselves, almost

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:12.230
every single day.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:14.690
"Can I Hug That?"

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:17.250
This is the difficult,
but squishy problem

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:19.790
that we're going to
be tackling today.

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:21.260
"Can I Hug That?"
is a system that

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:25.100
takes an image, it processes
it, and then tells you

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:28.330
if you should hug it.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:32.580
So for instance, let's
say we have this image

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:34.020
of an adorable fluffy bunny.

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:37.080
You say, "Absolutely,
I want to hug that.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:40.500
Maybe not too hard, it
looks rather fragile."

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:43.050
So "Can I Hug That?"
would say, "Go for it."

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.410
But if instead, I
gave it this image

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:47.400
of a shattered light bulb.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:48.870
It would say, "Back away slowly.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:51.630
I'm preemptively calling 911.

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:53.490
Just don't hug that one."

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:56.050
So this is easy for
us to understand,

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:58.480
but it's actually
quite difficult to do.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:01.240
If you thought about what
makes something huggable,

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:02.880
we would get into
this conversation

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.760
of the platonic ideal
of a huggable object,

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:10.360
there's no defining
characteristic.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.590
So what are we going to do.

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:17.370
Well, we're going to
take images that people

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:19.820
have found huggable
or not huggable,

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:21.940
and those are the labels.

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:26.100
And we're going to use
them to answer a question.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:27.430
Can I hug that?

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:28.670
And get an answer.

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:31.610
Hug or not.

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:32.770
So what do you think?

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:35.210
Is this going to be an
example of a regression

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:38.640
or a classification problem?

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:39.290
Yeah.

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:41.120
It's going to be a
classification problem.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:42.950
We're going to
train a classifier

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:47.830
to answer this question for us.

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:50.380
So let's go to a demo.

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:51.800
I'm going to head back.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:55.580
We're going to use
cloud machine learning

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:57.750
to train our classifier.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:01.740
And then to use it to
predict new images.

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:03.800
Thank you for
switching to the demo.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:06.470
So we have our example images.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:09.590
And I have them in my
cloud storage bucket here.

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:11.885
You can see, hug and not hug.

00:11:11.885 --> 00:11:20.970
And if I go into them, they
are just good old jpegs.

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:24.520
So I'll show you a
couple of examples.

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:30.570
So in my NOT_HUG category,
I have an image of a cactus.

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:35.330
I have hugged a cactus,
I do not recommend it.

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:41.510
But we also have things like
clouds, in our category.

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:46.210
If I could hug a cloud,
I definitely would.

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:49.760
So what we've done is we've
taken all of these images,

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:52.100
and we've transformed this
data set into something

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:54.590
that CloudML can process.

00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:58.250
And that turns it into
a bunch of JSON files.

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:05.490
So if I go into my
data, you can see

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:08.760
these are our JSON-test,
and JSON-train.

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:16.790
If we look at these
JSON files, then we

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:18.980
get something like this.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:24.040
It gives us the byte
list of our images.

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:26.290
And if we scroll
down a bit, I'm just

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:30.610
going to search because
this is a very large text,

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:33.230
for each image we also
have a label that's coming

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:37.720
from the HUG or NOT_HUG label.

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:39.450
And here we have--
it's telling us,

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:43.080
well, this particular
one is value one.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.780
So of course, we go back and
we say here's our dictionary,

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:49.740
and at value one,
we have NOT_HUG.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:52.060
So that particular byte
list was for an image

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:53.980
that was of category NOT_HUG.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:00.730
So once we have
all of this data,

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:05.010
we then can use it to
actually train our classifier.

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:09.430
So I'm going to switch
to the command line,

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:12.380
and I'm going to clear
it again, so as not

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:13.940
to give anything away.

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:17.640
And we've got a simple Python
script that lets us train.

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:22.060
But before I go, I'm
just going to show you

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:25.960
a couple of environment
variables that are necessary.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:31.740
So first, I've got
a few directories

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:33.970
to find-- where
we're going to put

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:37.260
the output, and a
temporary holding

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:41.880
directory on my local machine,
and where our data actually

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:43.400
exists, our input directory.

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:49.755
And then we also have
our model information.

00:13:49.755 --> 00:13:51.130
So we're using a
model that we've

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:54.790
created with CloudML,
called gpc_next,

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:58.480
and we're on version
4 of that model.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:01.840
So if we do python train.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:04.470
I'll just walk through
what we have here.

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:07.490
So first we have which model
we're using, the model name.

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:10.110
You can define a number
of different models.

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:12.280
The version that
we're working with.

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:14.240
Where our training files are.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:16.310
And where our test files are.

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:19.760
Now when you're training a
classifier or any system,

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:21.950
if you do not test and
validate the model,

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:25.660
then it's not really going to
be all that useful for you.

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:29.190
So we're taking 160
images of each category

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:31.590
to train the model.

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:35.770
And then using some to
test the model as well.

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:39.110
We're also using this
hyperparams file, which

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:43.450
goes into what is actually
happening with the system,

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:45.850
and I'll show you
that in just a minute.

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:48.270
Then when we output,
we'll output back

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:52.430
to Google Cloud Storage
in our output directory.

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:54.770
And we'll tell it the
project, and that we

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:57.210
want to run it in the cloud.

00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:03.310
So while I kick that off, I'll
show you the hyperparams file.

00:15:03.310 --> 00:15:06.490
So this tells us some
information about the network

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:09.220
that we're using to train.

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:11.070
So we know what the
learning rate is,

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:14.570
how many labels we
have, and how many

00:15:14.570 --> 00:15:21.020
lay hidden layers we have in
our neural network-- that's 512.

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:24.270
So our training job
has been kicked off,

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:25.770
and we can check
on it using gcloud.

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:34.450
And we've got a lot of jobs.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:37.830
You can see I was busy
testing the system earlier.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:41.060
This is the one we
just created, here.

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:43.020
And it's currently running.

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:44.045
We can check on it.

00:15:47.604 --> 00:15:52.930
And say, describe this one.

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:55.850
So this one isn't
quite done yet,

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:58.070
training takes a
tiny bit of time.

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:00.380
So I'm going to use one
that I've already trained

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:02.720
on this exact same data set.

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:06.270
I did this earlier today.

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.900
Now what I've done is I've
created a simple flask

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:15.829
server that takes an image and
then uses CloudML to give us

00:16:15.829 --> 00:16:16.370
a prediction.

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:22.290
So let's take an image.

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:24.060
I'm going to take an
image of an octopus.

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:25.940
We did not train on any octopus.

00:16:28.706 --> 00:16:30.330
And I'll show you
what that looks like.

00:16:33.809 --> 00:16:34.600
This is that image.

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:40.185
Now, if I click back
that was actually

00:16:40.185 --> 00:16:41.810
a pretty fast prediction.

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:43.100
So it says, "Don't hug this.

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:47.855
This is not something
you want to hug."

00:16:47.855 --> 00:16:50.540
But let's try something
a little bit different.

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:52.470
We're also we're going
to use another octopus.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:56.317
But we're going to use one
that is made out of the yarn,

00:16:56.317 --> 00:16:57.150
it's been crocheted.

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:03.520
And it looks like this one.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:08.400
We did train the
model on pillows.

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:09.867
So what's the prediction?

00:17:09.867 --> 00:17:11.700
Do you think we're going
to hug this or not?

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:16.740
And yes.

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:18.540
This is actually
something you would hug.

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:21.690
I find this really
interesting, because it's

00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:25.400
been trained to neither an
octopus or on stuffed animals.

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:29.580
But it's been able to
accurately predict whether you

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:31.790
would want to hug this or not.

00:17:31.790 --> 00:17:35.814
So let's head back to
the slides if I may.

00:17:35.814 --> 00:17:41.940
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:43.240
OK.

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:44.519
So that was CloudML.

00:17:44.519 --> 00:17:46.310
But maybe you're like
me, and that actually

00:17:46.310 --> 00:17:49.130
kind of pushed the boundaries
of your comfort zone.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:51.800
Let's take a look
at easy API's that

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:54.420
lets you add jolts
of intelligence where

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:55.310
you need them.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.480
So if you do know
machine learning then

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:02.200
you can use cloud
machine learning.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.830
But if you don't, you
can tap into models

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:08.120
that Google has trained over
extensive corporate data.

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:10.000
You don't have to provide
the training data,

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.810
you don't have to provide--
to tune any parameters.

00:18:12.810 --> 00:18:16.760
It's fully trained and
validated by Google.

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:19.610
One of them has been in
production for a good while

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:21.410
now, and that's
the Translate API.

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:24.380
It lets you tap into those
statistical language models

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:25.765
that I was talking
about earlier.

00:18:29.702 --> 00:18:31.660
And the new one, that
you heard about yesterday

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:33.740
is the Speech API.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:37.730
This lets you pass in just
audio data and a language.

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:41.090
And it will tell
you what words it

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:43.930
extracts from that audio data.

00:18:43.930 --> 00:18:47.710
You can get it all at
once or as it comes in.

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:50.060
The streaming mode is
very-- will be very

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:52.515
valuable for real-time systems.

00:18:56.470 --> 00:19:00.130
And the Vision API launched into
general availability yesterday.

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:03.870
But we actually launched it
into beta just in February.

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:09.170
You can use it to label images,
identify landmarks, logos,

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:10.450
do OCR.

00:19:10.450 --> 00:19:13.250
And what I find really cool
is the sentiment analysis

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:14.620
over images.

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:19.310
We've been able to do that
with text for a long time

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:21.467
but images are a little
bit harder, right.

00:19:21.467 --> 00:19:23.300
Sometimes I can't predict
whether somebody's

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:25.680
feeling angry or not.

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:28.090
So all you have to do is
throw it an encoded image

00:19:28.090 --> 00:19:31.060
or connect it to Google
Cloud Storage, which is great

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:34.490
if you want to do mass analysis
over a bucket of images,

00:19:34.490 --> 00:19:36.920
say maybe our hug data.

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:39.000
And it returns a
label and a score.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.250
That label is what it sees.

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:44.820
And that score is a
number between 0 and 1,

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:47.815
that indicates how certain it
is that that label is correct.

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:53.920
So now I'd like to
introduce David Zuckerman,

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:57.280
he's the Head of Developer
Experience at Wix.com

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:00.140
and he's going to talk
about their experience

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:03.256
with machine learning on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:20:03.256 --> 00:20:09.240
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:11.220
DAVID ZUCKERMAN:
Thank you, Julia.

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.120
Really excited to be
here today to tell you

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:15.450
a little bit about our story.

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:19.900
How Wix went from not using
machine learning at all,

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:22.270
not knowing anything
about machine learning,

00:20:22.270 --> 00:20:25.320
to doing something
very, very powerful.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:28.180
So like most of you in the
audience, when Julia asked,

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:30.480
"Raise your hand if you're
a machine learning expert."

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:32.190
I did not raise my hand.

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:34.410
Nor would most of the
people that we have

00:20:34.410 --> 00:20:37.710
working for us today with Wix.

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:41.110
When I think machine
learning, I think AlphaGo,

00:20:41.110 --> 00:20:45.390
I think autonomous vehicle's,
I think "Can I Hug That?"

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:49.970
I don't think site design,
which is what Wix does.

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:52.080
For those of you
who don't know Wix,

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:55.800
we are one of the largest
providers of web presence

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:56.740
on the internet.

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:59.080
We let people who don't know
anything about technology

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:02.849
build really
compelling websites.

00:21:02.849 --> 00:21:04.640
I don't think machine
learning here, right.

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:06.890
This is not something that
screams, "Wow, we're really

00:21:06.890 --> 00:21:08.060
advanced machine learning."

00:21:08.060 --> 00:21:09.500
Not that we don't
do cool things,

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:12.090
but it's not in the
machine learning category.

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:14.490
Unless when you saw our ad
in the Super Bowl of "Kung Fu

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:16.457
Panda", you thought,
"Yeah, so that company--

00:21:16.457 --> 00:21:18.040
that company really
knows what they're

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:19.940
doing around machine learning."

00:21:19.940 --> 00:21:23.330
So why am I my up here?

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:25.750
Why am I on stage
talking about something

00:21:25.750 --> 00:21:28.940
that obviously is not our forte?

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:31.630
To really understand
why I'm here,

00:21:31.630 --> 00:21:35.230
and why we're talking to you,
let's talk about Wix's business

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:35.730
model.

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:36.854
This might come as a shock.

00:21:36.854 --> 00:21:39.570
I know we're a startup, but we
actually have a business model.

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:44.150
The idea is that we come in,
you let anybody build a site.

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:47.540
And we've let 80 million
people build sites so far.

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:49.270
And we want them
eventually to pay us.

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:51.410
They don't have to,
we're a freemium model.

00:21:51.410 --> 00:21:53.730
But we'd like them
to hopefully give us

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:55.930
some money at some point
for either e-commerce

00:21:55.930 --> 00:22:00.770
or hosting or one of our
really cool applications.

00:22:00.770 --> 00:22:02.920
And something really
interesting happens

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:05.590
here, which is that
as we're trying

00:22:05.590 --> 00:22:08.030
to drive them to
convert-- the more we know

00:22:08.030 --> 00:22:10.640
about our user, the more
information we have,

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:14.740
the more we can figure out,
the better the experience.

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:18.670
But the more data we
collect about our users,

00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:20.650
the lower our conversion
rate is going to be.

00:22:20.650 --> 00:22:22.330
And this makes a
lot of sense, right.

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:24.825
If I'm designing a site.

00:22:24.825 --> 00:22:27.500
I've come in, I've
added an image gallery,

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:30.469
and I picked out
every single image,

00:22:30.469 --> 00:22:32.260
and I really spend a
lot of time with this.

00:22:32.260 --> 00:22:34.730
And then we popup dialogue
that says, "Hi, what are you

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:35.650
building?

00:22:35.650 --> 00:22:37.160
Tell me about your demographic.

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:39.510
Are you building a music site?"

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:42.660
It really turns off
our users, and it

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:45.800
drops in a lot of cases
our conversion rates.

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:48.209
So we're stuck in this
interesting position where

00:22:48.209 --> 00:22:49.750
we want to know more
about our users,

00:22:49.750 --> 00:22:51.560
but we don't want to ask.

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:54.080
But we want to convert.

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:55.630
But we can't ask.

00:22:55.630 --> 00:22:58.740
And so inevitably
when we sit around,

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:00.240
we always ask
basically, wouldn't it

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:02.890
be great to know
more about our users

00:23:02.890 --> 00:23:04.460
without having to ask them.

00:23:04.460 --> 00:23:07.560
And there's a lot we can do
in terms of historical data.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:09.785
We can infer and
we have inferred

00:23:09.785 --> 00:23:12.800
that a lot of our users
build e-commerce websites.

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:15.110
Or a lot of our
users are musicians,

00:23:15.110 --> 00:23:18.390
and would love a
music experience.

00:23:18.390 --> 00:23:20.890
Or hotels-- but this
is all historical.

00:23:20.890 --> 00:23:22.480
This is not in real-time.

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:24.420
And it's things
we have to infer.

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:28.480
And in a lot of cases,
it's a human or team

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:30.220
looking at sites
people have built

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:32.810
to figure out what's going on.

00:23:32.810 --> 00:23:35.895
And so inevitably, whenever
we're having this conversation,

00:23:35.895 --> 00:23:37.720
and I'll admit,
even though I know

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:40.170
almost nothing about
machine learning,

00:23:40.170 --> 00:23:43.702
inevitably someone says,
machine learning to the rescue.

00:23:43.702 --> 00:23:45.910
This is obviously going to
solve all of our problems.

00:23:45.910 --> 00:23:48.790
If we can drive a
car autonomously,

00:23:48.790 --> 00:23:50.870
we can definitely learn
more about our users

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:53.430
through machine learning.

00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:56.500
And then you get to this little
asterisk that-- basically,

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:59.810
does anyone at Wix know
anything about machine learning?

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:00.970
We don't.

00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:03.264
I'm very comfortable getting
on stage telling you this,

00:24:03.264 --> 00:24:05.430
we don't know a single thing
about machine learning.

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:07.617
It's not our core competency.

00:24:07.617 --> 00:24:08.950
We have other core competencies.

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:12.920
We have other things that we
are exceptionally good at.

00:24:12.920 --> 00:24:15.200
We know a lot about scalability.

00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:19.720
We serve 35 terabytes of
data every single day.

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:22.990
We hit-- we handle over
2 billion HTTP requests.

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:27.160
During the Super Bowl, when our
ops team was basically really

00:24:27.160 --> 00:24:28.260
concerned, "Oh my god.

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:30.510
The commercials going to
air, what's going to happen."

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:32.176
They ended up having
to watch a football

00:24:32.176 --> 00:24:33.930
game instead of
doing what they love,

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:35.990
which is putting out fires.

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:37.620
So scalability we get.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:38.870
We do a really great job here.

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:41.320
User behavior, we have
over 1,000 A/B tests

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:43.560
running in production
at any time.

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:46.100
We analyze this data, we
have giant Hadoop clusters

00:24:46.100 --> 00:24:48.910
that manage all this data
to try to gauge what people

00:24:48.910 --> 00:24:51.859
are doing historically again.

00:24:51.859 --> 00:24:53.400
This is something
we're very good at.

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:57.720
Also HTML editing products,
this is our core competency.

00:24:57.720 --> 00:24:59.490
We built four of them.

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:02.250
The current one is
probably the largest React

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:06.250
install anywhere outside
of Facebook or Instagram.

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:09.850
And we've put a massive UX and
usability investment into this.

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:12.240
These are our core competencies,
these are the things

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:14.090
that we hire for, and
these are the things

00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:15.480
that we're very good at.

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:18.384
Machine learning is
not in this list.

00:25:18.384 --> 00:25:20.050
We've got some really
great people also.

00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:21.860
We have over 250 engineers.

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:24.740
We've gotten to be
relatively good size company.

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:27.300
Over 40 product managers,
over 30 analysts

00:25:27.300 --> 00:25:30.460
and data scientists, but not a
single machine learning expert.

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:32.210
We have people that
dabble, we have people

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:34.290
that know what it
is, and know enough

00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:37.070
that we shouldn't
get involved in it.

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:40.980
But it's not just that we
don't have the right people.

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:42.790
It's also that machine
learning has been,

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:45.240
and you all know this, has
been a very, very expensive

00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:47.004
proposition.

00:25:47.004 --> 00:25:48.420
You have to hire
the right people,

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:51.003
you have to hire these experts,
the few people in the audience

00:25:51.003 --> 00:25:52.220
that raised their hands.

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:54.900
You have to find them,
you have to pay them.

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:58.610
You have to put in the
investment in the architecture

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:00.690
in the infrastructure.

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:02.090
And you have to
have enough data,

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:03.506
or hope that you
have enough data,

00:26:03.506 --> 00:26:06.360
that you can train these
models so that-- eventually

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:07.880
get what you're looking for.

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:10.050
And we're a start up.

00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:12.170
We can't necessarily
pay the cost

00:26:12.170 --> 00:26:15.930
of spending x amount of
money for all of these PhDs,

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:17.400
plus the money
and the investment

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:20.380
for something that might
not necessarily payoff.

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:24.170
I mean machine learning is still
in that category of science

00:26:24.170 --> 00:26:25.510
fiction.

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:27.880
It's still something that
seems out of the grasp of us

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:28.595
mere mortals.

00:26:32.190 --> 00:26:33.800
So when we think
about this, when

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:37.570
we think about machine learning
and how we could use it.

00:26:37.570 --> 00:26:39.550
We can go back and--
what is our oxygen,

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:42.340
what fuels-- what could possibly
fuel machine learning for us,

00:26:42.340 --> 00:26:45.160
even if we were to think
about going down that path.

00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:47.220
And for us, it's images.

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:49.240
We have over two
petabytes of images

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:51.154
that our users
have uploaded to us

00:26:51.154 --> 00:26:52.820
over the course of
our existence, that's

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:54.990
a lot of data.

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:57.970
And imagine if we could
mine this data for something

00:26:57.970 --> 00:27:02.850
interesting, the possibilities
here are basically endless.

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:04.480
We could recommend
similar photos

00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:07.650
from Bigstock, one of our
stock photo providers.

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:09.910
We could pre-build sites
for target verticals.

00:27:09.910 --> 00:27:13.790
If we ask one question,
"What are you building?"

00:27:13.790 --> 00:27:16.940
We could dynamically produce a
website with the right images,

00:27:16.940 --> 00:27:20.260
and the web is very
visual, and build the user

00:27:20.260 --> 00:27:22.480
a really compelling experience.

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:25.490
We could make it easier
for users to actually find

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:28.250
images in the first place,
which is definitely a pain

00:27:28.250 --> 00:27:30.279
point that we've seen.

00:27:30.279 --> 00:27:32.320
We could know what our
existing users have built.

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:34.278
And not know in the sense
that we're inferring,

00:27:34.278 --> 00:27:37.230
but actually know
what's in these images,

00:27:37.230 --> 00:27:41.450
and provide a better
experience either in real-time

00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:43.860
or for future users.

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:46.660
So all of this has
been impossible.

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:50.640
There's been no way that we
would think about doing this.

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:52.360
It had been.

00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:54.750
So enter the Vision API.

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:58.530
We were lucky enough to get
early access to these APIs.

00:27:58.530 --> 00:28:01.640
And we immediately sat
around and thought, OK, we

00:28:01.640 --> 00:28:03.920
could do all of these
really compelling things.

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:08.480
But let's not get excited, let's
see what we can actually do.

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:11.740
And so we handed it off to
one of our junior engineers.

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:15.360
And said, OK here's
the API documentation,

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:18.110
you know the system,
see what you can do.

00:28:18.110 --> 00:28:20.484
She came back like
15 minutes later,

00:28:20.484 --> 00:28:22.150
and we were expecting
two or three days,

00:28:22.150 --> 00:28:23.680
and she came back
15 minutes later

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:27.320
and showed off some
pretty amazing things.

00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:29.410
To the point that
even before the API

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:32.590
was out of beta, even
before the Vision API was

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:34.090
released to the
public, we started

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:36.140
rolling it into production.

00:28:36.140 --> 00:28:37.630
So what do we do?

00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:42.680
All of the images that
are uploaded to us,

00:28:42.680 --> 00:28:46.040
and these are over two
million daily images,

00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:47.830
we extract labels for.

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:51.760
Every single thing that our user
uploads to Wix at this point,

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:53.540
we extract.

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:57.440
We've also taken all over
existing user photos--

00:28:57.440 --> 00:28:59.330
everything, all these
two petabytes of data,

00:28:59.330 --> 00:29:02.890
including all of the
stock photos that we have.

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:04.270
And we've analyzed all of them.

00:29:04.270 --> 00:29:06.010
This is actually
really convenient.

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:10.180
We actually store our images on
Google Cloud Storage, anyway.

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:12.150
So we're on the Google
backbone, we basically

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:14.650
pointed Vision
API at our bucket,

00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:16.897
and it went ahead and
did all the other work.

00:29:16.897 --> 00:29:18.480
So that means that
all of these images

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:20.282
are now searchable by our users.

00:29:20.282 --> 00:29:21.990
A user can come in
when they add a site--

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:23.710
and I'll show you
this in a second--

00:29:23.710 --> 00:29:24.710
actually find something.

00:29:24.710 --> 00:29:26.190
It sounds so obvious.

00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:27.980
Of course, image search.

00:29:27.980 --> 00:29:30.240
But image search, when you
really think technically,

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:35.080
what it entails, is near--
has always been impossible.

00:29:35.080 --> 00:29:37.400
So we've also added
face detection as well.

00:29:37.400 --> 00:29:39.600
So we take-- for every
image that's uploaded,

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:40.580
we take out the faces.

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:43.360
We extract that into EXIF data.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:45.720
And we are able to add
things like face cropping

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:46.790
all across our platform.

00:29:46.790 --> 00:29:51.890
Both in the Wix editor itself,
and also in the media hosting

00:29:51.890 --> 00:29:55.250
platform we have called WixMP.

00:29:55.250 --> 00:29:57.690
So let's switch to the
demo machine please.

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:08.130
Can I get the switch please?

00:30:08.130 --> 00:30:09.440
Thank you.

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:14.160
All right, so I wanted to build
a quick little site to remember

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:15.610
my experience GCP Next.

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:17.620
And I'm not a designer.

00:30:17.620 --> 00:30:20.080
I know what looks good, but
I cannot design it to save

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:20.580
my life.

00:30:20.580 --> 00:30:22.110
So I did what most
of our users do,

00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:24.340
which is to take a template.

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:27.350
And you can see this image of
someone doing yoga on a cliff,

00:30:27.350 --> 00:30:30.480
while very beautiful, doesn't
really scream GCP Next,

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:32.640
doesn't really
scream San Francisco.

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:35.430
So I'm going to go ahead and
do what our users would do,

00:30:35.430 --> 00:30:37.630
which is change the image.

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:40.270
And we get this media
manager that comes up.

00:30:40.270 --> 00:30:42.120
You can see I've
uploaded one image.

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:44.215
It's not even remotely
of San Francisco.

00:30:44.215 --> 00:30:46.340
And so let's put yourself
in the mindset of a user,

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:48.006
or any of you who are
coming to do this,

00:30:48.006 --> 00:30:49.750
you want an image
of San Francisco.

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:52.500
You'll probably open
another browser tab,

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:55.160
you'll search for San
Francisco, you'll find an image,

00:30:55.160 --> 00:30:57.170
you'll save it,
you'll download it,

00:30:57.170 --> 00:30:59.300
you'll then reupload it to us.

00:30:59.300 --> 00:31:01.650
For a lot of our users,
they're not technical.

00:31:01.650 --> 00:31:04.660
This might actually be something
that trips them up to the point

00:31:04.660 --> 00:31:07.900
that they will not
continue building a site.

00:31:07.900 --> 00:31:12.390
So now let's take
advantage of image search.

00:31:12.390 --> 00:31:14.550
So I'm going to search
for San Francisco.

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:19.980
And I get some really
interesting results here.

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:23.300
I have a map that actually has
a pin that says San Francisco.

00:31:23.300 --> 00:31:27.680
I have a cable car, I have the
bridge, I have the Golden Gate.

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:31.650
I have a hotel that's inside--
that's somewhere in the city.

00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:34.060
These are not just
obvious things that

00:31:34.060 --> 00:31:35.380
say San Francisco somewhere.

00:31:35.380 --> 00:31:38.092
These are actually really
well analyzed images.

00:31:38.092 --> 00:31:39.550
So now from the
user's perspective,

00:31:39.550 --> 00:31:42.276
I can decide I
really like this one.

00:31:42.276 --> 00:31:46.230
And I can go ahead and
I can add it to my site.

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:47.980
And immediately
something that used

00:31:47.980 --> 00:31:50.110
to be incredibly
hard for users just

00:31:50.110 --> 00:31:53.010
becomes easy, without
them knowing anything

00:31:53.010 --> 00:31:54.170
about what we've done.

00:31:54.170 --> 00:31:55.870
Can we go back to
the slides, please?

00:32:01.960 --> 00:32:07.160
So the next thing that I'm
going to talk about and show you

00:32:07.160 --> 00:32:08.420
is WixMP.

00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:12.060
WixMP is Wix's media
distribution platform.

00:32:12.060 --> 00:32:15.290
All of the imagery resizing, all
the image distribution-- audio,

00:32:15.290 --> 00:32:17.230
video, everything
that we do, and these

00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:20.770
are part of that 35 terabytes
of data that we serve every day,

00:32:20.770 --> 00:32:22.090
is a service that we resell.

00:32:22.090 --> 00:32:24.048
It's a platform as a
service, and it's actually

00:32:24.048 --> 00:32:25.110
running on GCP.

00:32:25.110 --> 00:32:29.750
Wix is the largest
tenant on this platform.

00:32:29.750 --> 00:32:33.130
And this platform also gains
labels and face detection.

00:32:33.130 --> 00:32:33.690
Why?

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:35.360
Because we do.

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:36.210
Because Wix does.

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:38.340
Because we're just
calling an API.

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:42.720
And we're pushing this down to
users of our platform as well.

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:45.250
It's one of the powerful things
about platforms and APIs,

00:32:45.250 --> 00:32:45.950
right.

00:32:45.950 --> 00:32:48.770
You can mix and match and you
can easily give functionality

00:32:48.770 --> 00:32:51.420
to anybody down the
chain without really

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:55.109
having to do a lot of work.

00:32:55.109 --> 00:32:56.650
So I've shown you
what it looks like,

00:32:56.650 --> 00:32:58.110
and you've heard
us talk about it.

00:32:58.110 --> 00:32:59.875
But let me show you the code.

00:32:59.875 --> 00:33:01.875
Can we switch to the demo
machine again, please?

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:12.502
So let me ask a
different question.

00:33:12.502 --> 00:33:14.710
Julie asked before, how many
machine learning experts

00:33:14.710 --> 00:33:15.834
do we have in the audience.

00:33:15.834 --> 00:33:20.180
How many of you can
call a REST API?

00:33:20.180 --> 00:33:23.750
OK, kind of thought that
would be most of the audience.

00:33:23.750 --> 00:33:26.900
So here is our from
production code that

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:29.222
does all of the dirty work.

00:33:29.222 --> 00:33:31.180
And you'll notice that,
as I walk through this,

00:33:31.180 --> 00:33:33.100
we have about five lines
of code that are actually

00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:34.850
calling the Vision
API, and the rest of it

00:33:34.850 --> 00:33:38.960
is basically Python scaffolding.

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:40.870
So we set the API.

00:33:40.870 --> 00:33:41.700
It's very simple.

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:45.440
It's a-- if you want images,
give it our key, which sorry,

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:48.150
I'm not going to
show our key here.

00:33:48.150 --> 00:33:50.240
We go ahead and we
pass in an image URL.

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:53.700
And from that URL
or the image data,

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:56.000
we call a built-in
Base64 library.

00:33:56.000 --> 00:33:59.050
And we encode the
image to Base64.

00:33:59.050 --> 00:34:01.210
Then we get to the really
challenging part, which

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:03.270
is constructing a JSON request.

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:05.300
That tells us which
information we actually want

00:34:05.300 --> 00:34:07.340
to extract from this image.

00:34:07.340 --> 00:34:10.139
So we decided we want face
detection, label detection,

00:34:10.139 --> 00:34:12.210
landmark detection,
and logo detection.

00:34:12.210 --> 00:34:14.630
So we're going all
out here, basically.

00:34:14.630 --> 00:34:19.929
And in this JSON, we also put
the Base64 encoded image data.

00:34:19.929 --> 00:34:23.225
So this is preparing
the request.

00:34:23.225 --> 00:34:25.350
And you can see that when
we actually call execute,

00:34:25.350 --> 00:34:28.139
all we're doing is
using an HTTP library,

00:34:28.139 --> 00:34:31.230
and executing and waiting
for the response, that's it.

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:34.280
There's no rocket science here,
there's no advanced computer

00:34:34.280 --> 00:34:36.840
science here, it's
calling a REST API.

00:34:36.840 --> 00:34:41.179
And it was that simple to
gain huge benefits, things

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:44.830
that we could not do otherwise.

00:34:44.830 --> 00:34:47.770
So I want to show you
one other thing here.

00:34:47.770 --> 00:34:50.889
So this is a node
library for WixMP.

00:34:50.889 --> 00:34:54.199
WixMP again, is our immediate
distribution platform.

00:34:54.199 --> 00:34:56.770
And it's a simple
node API, and all

00:34:56.770 --> 00:34:58.620
it does is it uploads
a couple images.

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:01.980
So I wanted to try
something interesting.

00:35:01.980 --> 00:35:04.866
So I took this image of
San Francisco in 2005,

00:35:04.866 --> 00:35:06.560
it's been here for a long time.

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:09.190
There's no EXIF data,
there's no GPS data,

00:35:09.190 --> 00:35:10.790
there's nothing encoded in here.

00:35:10.790 --> 00:35:12.660
I wanted to see if
the Image API was

00:35:12.660 --> 00:35:18.575
going to recognize San
Francisco, even 2005--

00:35:18.575 --> 00:35:20.700
11 years ago.

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:24.299
And Julia and I, before
this, we took a selfie.

00:35:24.299 --> 00:35:26.340
Because, of course, you're
giving a presentation,

00:35:26.340 --> 00:35:28.140
you have to take a selfie.

00:35:28.140 --> 00:35:30.270
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:33.250
going to upload both of
these images to the API.

00:35:33.250 --> 00:35:36.590
And as you can see, it's
about as easy as possible.

00:35:36.590 --> 00:35:38.720
Upload, pass in the
name of the file.

00:35:38.720 --> 00:35:40.160
So let's kick off both of these.

00:35:44.330 --> 00:35:48.830
Upload SF and upload the selfie.

00:35:48.830 --> 00:35:52.220
And in honor of Julia's
should I hug this or not,

00:35:52.220 --> 00:35:53.720
I have a little
custom loader that's

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:56.250
going between bunnies and cacti.

00:35:56.250 --> 00:35:58.710
I'm sure the classifier would
have a field day with this.

00:35:58.710 --> 00:36:00.210
But so what is this
doing right now?

00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:04.990
So this is uploading two
images to Wix, to WixMP.

00:36:04.990 --> 00:36:08.060
It's taking them and then WixMP,
while it's persisting things

00:36:08.060 --> 00:36:11.100
to cloud storage and while
it's extracting and copying,

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:13.580
it's also going ahead
and it's sending it off

00:36:13.580 --> 00:36:18.300
to Google to tell us what's
actually in this image.

00:36:18.300 --> 00:36:21.770
And let's take a look
at our from 2005 image.

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:23.520
Water, sea, sure.

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:27.780
Landform, bridge, waterway,
Oakland Bay Bridge,

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:31.420
San Francisco Oakland Bay
Bridge, and San Francisco.

00:36:31.420 --> 00:36:33.420
It's really fascinating
to me that, OK, sure.

00:36:33.420 --> 00:36:35.670
You know, a human might look
at this and say, oh yeah,

00:36:35.670 --> 00:36:36.280
it's a bridge.

00:36:36.280 --> 00:36:38.440
It's just the Bay Bridge.

00:36:38.440 --> 00:36:41.640
But because you have this giant
trained model in the cloud,

00:36:41.640 --> 00:36:44.240
it also realizes
water, sea, landform,

00:36:44.240 --> 00:36:46.640
and it picks out a little
bit of San Francisco.

00:36:46.640 --> 00:36:48.670
If you didn't know that
that was the Bay Bridge,

00:36:48.670 --> 00:36:50.795
you'd have no way of knowing
that that was actually

00:36:50.795 --> 00:36:51.940
San Francisco.

00:36:51.940 --> 00:36:53.900
And then obviously,
for labels, it's

00:36:53.900 --> 00:36:57.780
good to see that the machine
thinks Julia and I are people.

00:36:57.780 --> 00:37:01.140
And you can see that we've got
a couple of faces from the API.

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:06.010
And if we go to the Wix
Media Platform console,

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:08.870
you can see that we can go
back and forth between turning

00:37:08.870 --> 00:37:10.520
on the face detection or not.

00:37:10.520 --> 00:37:12.200
Face detection is pretty good.

00:37:12.200 --> 00:37:14.547
Also takes our tag here.

00:37:14.547 --> 00:37:16.255
If I could go back to
the slides, please.

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:25.290
So we basically, we
did the impossible.

00:37:25.290 --> 00:37:27.400
We did-- or what would
have been impossible

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:28.850
even two or three years ago.

00:37:28.850 --> 00:37:31.480
We harnessed machine learning,
without really knowing anything

00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:32.880
about machine learning.

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:35.772
We now know more
about our users,

00:37:35.772 --> 00:37:36.980
without asking them anything.

00:37:36.980 --> 00:37:40.530
This is the holy grail
for this kind of work,

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:43.256
for consumer facing products.

00:37:43.256 --> 00:37:44.880
And now that we've
gotten our feet wet.

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:49.540
Now that we realize that the
Vision API is within our grasp,

00:37:49.540 --> 00:37:51.610
and other APIs are
within our grasp,

00:37:51.610 --> 00:37:54.315
and now that we know
that we have enough data,

00:37:54.315 --> 00:37:56.440
and now that we know that
machine learning actually

00:37:56.440 --> 00:37:59.650
pays off, we're looking
at CloudML as well.

00:37:59.650 --> 00:38:01.850
I'd love to put the
"Can I Hug This or Not"

00:38:01.850 --> 00:38:04.192
as a hidden feature in Wix.

00:38:04.192 --> 00:38:05.650
But we're looking
at different ways

00:38:05.650 --> 00:38:07.190
that we can take
advantage of this.

00:38:07.190 --> 00:38:09.960
We could see which
layouts people like,

00:38:09.960 --> 00:38:12.210
and start auto laying out sites.

00:38:12.210 --> 00:38:15.240
There's so much potential when
you harness machine learning.

00:38:15.240 --> 00:38:18.000
And the great
thing about this is

00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:20.200
it's the democratization
of machine learning.

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:21.940
It's bringing it
down to everybody.

00:38:21.940 --> 00:38:25.640
Either from the API side,
if you can call a REST API,

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:26.750
you can use this.

00:38:26.750 --> 00:38:30.034
Or the CloudML side, that you
can know a little bit more

00:38:30.034 --> 00:38:31.450
without having to
be a researcher.

00:38:31.450 --> 00:38:33.840
It's really amazing.

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:36.085
And with that, I'd love to
hand it back off to Julia.

00:38:36.085 --> 00:38:38.460
And she will walk you through
the rest of the cool stuff.

00:38:38.460 --> 00:38:46.530
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:46.530 --> 00:38:48.050
JULIA FERRAIOLI:
Thank you, David.

00:38:48.050 --> 00:38:49.990
You're stealing my
talking points, though.

00:38:49.990 --> 00:38:52.560
We're going to talk
about that later.

00:38:52.560 --> 00:38:56.920
So you really heard
so much about machine

00:38:56.920 --> 00:38:58.740
learning yesterday and today.

00:38:58.740 --> 00:39:03.290
And we have these easy
APIs that you do everything

00:39:03.290 --> 00:39:09.920
from understanding language
to understanding vision

00:39:09.920 --> 00:39:11.440
and understanding audio.

00:39:11.440 --> 00:39:14.020
We're kind of covering
the range here.

00:39:14.020 --> 00:39:16.490
And like David said,
all you need to do

00:39:16.490 --> 00:39:19.150
is be able to make an API call.

00:39:19.150 --> 00:39:23.780
It's as simple as
it can get here.

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:27.330
And this is really the
year of machine learning.

00:39:27.330 --> 00:39:29.290
We're seeing an uptick
in news stories.

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:31.320
We're seeing an
uptick in how people

00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:33.350
are making intelligent systems.

00:39:33.350 --> 00:39:37.020
I'm always impressed with
what's going on in the industry

00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:37.810
right now.

